{
  "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
  "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:03 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0000-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "PAUL W. HUGHES, ESQUIRE, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Petitioner."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HUGHES: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The court of appeals erred in dismissing Petitioner's improper fact-finding claim in three separate ways."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The way the government envisions this statute is a litigant would have to first file a petition for review of the final order of removal, but, at the same time, the government believes, while you're filing that petition for review to have a timely petition for review, your claims are simultaneously unexhausted and you must at -- at -- at that same time go back to the -- the -- the Board and ask for a motion to reconsider."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So the -- the government's opposition brief at page 13, they acknowledged at the certiorari stage that waiver and forfeiture would apply in this case. I think that acknowledgment was -- was right then."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Can I just ask you about whether or not we need to opine on the issue exhaustion versus remedy exhaustion if we agree with you about \"as of right\"?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Dubin",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The INA creates an adversarial scheme that authorizes judicial review only after agency procedures are exhausted. That reflects Congress's judgment on how best to manage the high volume of immigration cases to achieve uniformity, efficiency, and fairness in an overburdened system."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Can you give us an example of another provision where \"as of right,\" the phrase \"as of right,\" is used to describe a discretionary motion?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: But it didn't -- it didn't codify the exact language."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Dubin",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. DUBIN: I don't think there's a plausible argument, and -- and the reason is because of what changes Congress made from 1105 to 1252(d)(1). All Congress did in the relevant part of the statute was omit -- was change from a passive voice and a double negative."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE JACKSON: Right. But, in any event, neither of those say the language that appears everywhere else when the court is really speaking to jurisdiction, right? Like is it your suggestion that when the Court said \"no court shall have jurisdiction to review,\" which it says many, many times, you think that the Court -- that the Congress was using interchangeably that language and the one in our statute, both to be referring to jurisdiction?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Dubin",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0002-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. DUBIN: Yes. But the Court has referred to that requirement in the Social Security Act as an exhaustion requirement. It's not the exhaust --the type of exhaustion requirement we have here, but it's an exhaustion requirement that the Court has found continues to be jurisdictional post-Arbaugh."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: Can you describe what you think an impermissible fact-finding claim is when it's successful?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. DUBIN: I think it's when the Board finds facts that are -- that the immigration judge didn't consider and -- and uses that to make -- and rests its decision on that. So I think what -- why that's not the case here is because what the Board did is reweigh the same facts the immigration judge considered but this time with the -- with the presumption that the immigration judge found didn't apply."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE JACKSON: Can I ask you, getting back a little bit to the jurisdictional, I guess I'm a little worried that the -- the fact that these exhaustion provision is directed at the court may not necessarily be indicative of its jurisdictional character."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. DUBIN: I think provisions that are directed to what the court must do is what this Court has been looking for in this series of cases. That's what the court said --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0003-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 75,
        "start": 75
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "As to \"as of right,\" we just don't think the government has any role for that statutory text that isn't already captured by the separate term \"available.\" And in many exhaustion provisions, Congress spoke about available remedies, but, here, Congress added more, added \"as of right,\" and we think that statutory language has to have a purpose, and only Petitioner gives that language meaning."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1436",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1c0b8504ef7b5036a3e37a2b0e987a2703a806724dd38aae6e2e02e4095425cd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1c0b8504ef7b-0003-0005",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 77,
        "start": 77
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
    {
